shi:
A woman's words and slanders bring worry and alarm;
the filial son arrives at the door to the sound of weeping.
When fortune has withdrawn, even gold loses its luster;
brows unfurled in grief, nothing can be accomplished.

There is another road beside the river's sands;
do not follow a woman on her wanderings.
Do not lament the many hindrances and delays;
once you attain your aim, all is ease and grace.

A woman stands before the door;
in the end good fortune comes.
From now on there are no perils or hindrances;
enjoy your blessings, and harbor no doubt or suspicion.

Hidden affairs emerge from a dark chamber;
bewildering clouds seal up the inner room.
The one of the joined branches cannot be seen;
I am left alone in solitary sorrow.

Gazing till my eyes fail for the traveler, his affairs grow hollow;
though we meet upon the road, we have become estranged.
Then he promised to fulfill the plan of a lifetime;
but waiting until now, I have not seen him at all.

Do not say a small matter is merely as it seems;
for dread that, drifting on, it will grow hard.
If, unheeding, you tread the frost, it leads to overthrow;
when the ice turns hard, you may suffer hurt and ruin.

At home there is much to celebrate;
in wealth and honor one attains peace and safety.
The ten thousand affairs spring up from the heart;
all of them turn toward what is good.

Treasured horses and noble steeds throng in procession;
they startle the lake and hills from their noonday sleep.
For a thousand li all is easy, with no hindrance;
green waves and fragrant grasses stretch to the horizon.

Wishing to walk a level road, you meet trouble instead;
if you seek fame and profit, better to stay at leisure.
In the midst of worry, joy meets a good physician's hand;
and so the remnant of your life is kept here in the world.

The tryst is but a dream;
without the destined bond, few affairs succeed.
The old friend is not to be met;
a thousand li away, the moon shines bright in vain.

Bright clouds scatter easily and can hardly be kept;
in the lonely dusk, affairs are fit for sorrow.
Only secret merit aids you in the dark;
yet even so you will grow wan and carry care at heart.

Good fortune draws near the gate and door;
a golden rooster leaps in the sun's glow.
Yin and yang just now aid one another;
you return alone, riding a many-hued phoenix.

Nobles gather in throngs before the door;
within the household, joyful airs abound.
Profit and emolument by nature form wealth and honor;
the principles of all one's life are brought to full completion.

The pent-up gloom is wholly dispelled;
spring comes and the myriad things flourish.
Fields and silkworms favor your household;
plowing and tilling bring joy to the common people.

Fortune meets squarely with its season;
cloud follows dragon, wind follows tiger.
Holding to the common good, the emblems of office are yours;
your renown reaches the palace of the sovereign.

A noble person aids you in the dark;
what is stored and used are well matched.
Do not wonder that the flowering branches are few;
know that the fruit comes only late.

Sitting alone in the high tower, I recall old wanderings;
the moon is bright as day, and the water flows east.
The promise of those years should not have been broken;
and so, when we meet, our laughter does not cease.

Beware the time when kindred turn against each other;
those who were ever close have changed to discord.
Then the rotted money-strings sit empty and unused;
the grasses on the riverbank grow thick and even.

The sport of shooting the deer arises in a moment,
but it must be brought about by the strength of many.
The peach of immortality is now ripe;
lift your eyes, and at last enjoy the glory.
Again: the deer runs and the multitude speeds after it;
merit and fame are in this very hour.
The crafty tyrants are now brought to an end;
the four seas are bathed in harmony and peace.

Clouds thin, the toad's light grows dim;
flowers open, half spoiled by rain.
Exorcise and remove, and in the end gain blessing;
retire in quiet and seek after peace.

The lone yang is faint;
the host of yin steals upon it.
Its strength is spent;
it will not reach old age.
Be wary! Be wary!
You should preserve yourself.

Gold, refined a hundred times, alone keeps its true nature;
white jade, cast aside thrice, only then shows its worth.
Stored in its casket, it is not yet admired by men;
content in willing retreat, one rests at ease in poverty.

The ten thousand affairs are not by man's will;
sorrow and joy rest in a single body.
Guard against the faint and stop up the gradual;
in ordering affairs, do not drift along in habit.

Relying on wealth, you forget your root;
in greed's excess, you lose the mean.
With a deceiving heart, Heaven does not protect you;
the way of the spirits does not favor selfish deeds.
